T12.01|Shareholder value is the value of owners' residual claims, supported by sustainable business cash flows and risk. Maximising a short-term earnings metric can conflict with long-term value if it sacrifices investment, customer trust or resilience.~~Value creation requires returns exceeding the opportunity cost of capital, while respecting contractual, legal and stakeholder constraints. A rise in profit without considering capital committed can give the wrong incentive.|Earnings objective: Period accounting result.;;Value objective: Risk-adjusted present value of owner benefits.;;Operational objective: Drivers supporting durable cash creation.|Project A earns ₹15 lakh NOPAT on ₹100 capital; B earns ₹20 on ₹250. Assume WACC 10%.~~A economic profit = ₹15 − ₹10 = ₹5.;;B economic profit = ₹20 − ₹25 = −₹5.;;Higher earnings in B do not compensate for its larger capital requirement.~~Absolute profit and value creation can rank investments differently.~~Forecast duration and capital recovery matter in a full NPV assessment.|Identify the objective and horizon.;;Compare returns with capital cost.;;Assess durable cash and obligations.
T12.02|Stakeholder trade-offs arise because owners, employees, customers, suppliers, creditors and communities face different effects. Agency costs arise when decision-makers' incentives diverge from those they represent. Contract design and governance seek workable alignment rather than eliminating every conflict.~~Ignoring stakeholder responses can undermine owner value through disruption, reputation or legal consequences. Conversely, spending described as beneficial still needs measurable outcomes and resource discipline.|Owner-manager conflict: Incentive divergence in delegated decisions.;;Creditor-owner conflict: Risk choices redistribute claim value.;;Stakeholder externality: Costs or benefits fall outside the decision-maker's accounts.|A project increases equity upside but raises lender downside without consent under existing terms.~~Identify how downside losses and upside gains are allocated.;;Read covenants and contractual restrictions.;;Assess total project value separately from value transferred among claimants.~~A shareholder gain can partly represent a transfer from creditors.~~Fair and enforceable agreements can price these risks; hidden risk shifting can damage future access.|Map affected parties.;;Separate creation from transfer.;;Assess incentives, contracts and long-run consequences.
T12.03|Investment chooses uses of capital, financing chooses funding claims and payout decides whether cash stays in the business or returns to owners. These decisions interact: payout can reduce flexibility, debt can constrain future investment and a project can change the firm's risk.~~Retained cash is justified by worthwhile opportunities and resilience needs, not simply because management prefers a larger company. Cheap-looking funding does not rescue an intrinsically weak project without a coherent financing benefit.|Investment: Acquire or develop cash-generating resources.;;Financing: Choose debt, equity and other funding.;;Payout: Dividends, repurchases or retention.|A firm has ₹50 lakh surplus cash, a positive-NPV project requiring ₹30 and a ₹10 contingency need. Assume the estimates are defensible.~~Allocate ₹30 to the project.;;Retain ₹10 for the defined buffer.;;Evaluate returning the remaining ₹10 or another worthwhile use.~~Investment and resilience needs frame the payout decision.~~Debt maturity, tax and ownership effects can change the actual choice.|Evaluate uses before funding labels.;;Map liquidity and constraints.;;Compare retention with owner alternatives.
T12.04|Capital budgeting evaluates long-term investments through incremental cash flows, risk and alternatives. The process includes identification, forecasting, valuation, approval and post-investment review. Cash investment, operating benefit, taxes, working capital and terminal recovery all matter.~~A spreadsheet's positive NPV is not evidence that demand or implementation assumptions are correct. Validate drivers and test downside before committing irreversible resources.|Expansion project: Add capacity or market reach.;;Replacement project: Substitute assets or processes.;;Mandatory or strategic project: Meet requirements or preserve options.|A project costs ₹100 lakh and pays ₹60 at each of two year-ends, with 10% required return and no other flows.~~Receipt PV = 60/1.1 + 60/1.1² = ₹104.13.;;NPV = ₹4.13.;;Stress lower receipts or delayed opening because the cushion is small.~~The model creates modest value under the base assumptions.~~A complete proposal also needs operational feasibility and explicit omitted costs.|Use dated incremental flows.;;Validate business assumptions.;;Define approval and later performance review.
T12.05|Incremental cash flows are the changes caused by choosing a project rather than the relevant alternative. Include additional revenue, avoidable costs, working capital, tax and opportunity costs. Exclude allocated costs that do not actually change.~~The comparison baseline must be credible. New-product sales that replace existing sales are not wholly incremental, and using an owned building can forgo rent even without a new invoice.|Direct incremental flow: New receipt or payment caused by the choice.;;Cannibalisation: Existing benefit displaced by the project.;;Opportunity cost: Feasible alternative benefit sacrificed.|A new product adds ₹30 lakh annual contribution but displaces ₹10 from an existing product. Assume no other changes.~~Gross new contribution is ₹30.;;Subtract displaced contribution ₹10.;;Incremental contribution is ₹20 before other project effects.~~Counting all new-product earnings overstates the decision benefit.~~Include launch spending, working capital and capital investment separately in the cash-flow model.|Define the alternative case.;;Include displacement and avoidable costs.;;Exclude unchanged allocations and sunk spending.
T12.06|Sunk costs have already been incurred and cannot be changed by the current decision. Opportunity costs are benefits sacrificed by choosing one use over another. Externalities are effects on other activities or parties that may matter to the evaluation boundary.~~A desire to recover past spending does not make future losses worthwhile. But information learned from that spending can change the forecast, and legal or contractual consequences can be relevant future flows.|Sunk cost: Unrecoverable past expenditure.;;Opportunity cost: Best feasible alternative benefit forgone.;;External effect: Impact on other operations or stakeholders.|A firm spent ₹10 lakh on research. It now can invest ₹20 and receive ₹25 discounted benefits, while giving up ₹8 from another use of the site.~~Exclude the sunk ₹10 from the new-choice NPV.;;Include the ₹8 opportunity cost.;;Incremental NPV = ₹25 − ₹20 − ₹8 = −₹3.~~Ignoring sunk cost is correct, but ignoring opportunity cost would reverse the conclusion.~~Assess only feasible alternatives and avoid double counting their benefits.|Separate past from changeable flows.;;Identify realistic alternatives.;;Include relevant spillovers within the stated boundary.
T12.07|Independent projects can be undertaken separately; mutually exclusive projects compete for the same need or resource. Replacement compares keeping the existing asset with changing it, including sale value, maintenance, efficiency and tax effects. Expansion compares incremental capacity with a feasible base case.~~Different lives and service outputs complicate comparisons. Repetition assumptions, equivalent annual cost or a common horizon may be needed instead of simply choosing the smallest initial price.|Independent: Choices do not preclude one another.;;Mutually exclusive: Only one alternative can be selected.;;Replacement versus expansion: Change an existing resource or add new activity.|Old equipment costs ₹15 lakh annual operating cash; a new machine costs ₹30 now and reduces annual cost to ₹5 for five years. Ignore tax and salvage.~~Annual saving is ₹10.;;At 10%, saving PV is about ₹37.91.;;Replacement NPV = ₹37.91 − ₹30 = ₹7.91.~~Savings, not the new machine's total revenue, drive this comparison.~~Include disposal value and downtime when present.|Define the keep-or-change baseline.;;Match service and horizons.;;Include transition, disposal and operating effects.
T12.08|Investment metrics answer different questions. NPV estimates currency value; IRR estimates a break-even discount rate; MIRR uses specified financing and reinvestment conventions; payback measures recovery speed; profitability index scales value against an investment base.~~Rate and speed rankings can conflict with value rankings. Capital constraints may make scaled measures useful, but project indivisibility and timing still matter.|NPV: Absolute present value added.;;IRR or MIRR: Defined percentage-return measure.;;Payback: Time to recover investment.;;Profitability index: Relative value per defined investment unit.|Project A costs ₹10 and yields PV benefits ₹15; B costs ₹100 and yields PV ₹130.~~NPVs are ₹5 and ₹30.;;Benefit/cost indices are 1.5 and 1.3.;;With sufficient resources and mutually exclusive comparable choices, B adds more value; tight funding can change feasibility.~~Higher relative attractiveness does not always mean greater total value.~~State the index convention and actual constraints.|Use NPV as a value reference.;;Understand each metric's limitations.;;Compare feasible combinations and timing.
T12.09|Equivalent annual cost converts a project's present-value cost into a constant annual amount over its service life. It can compare alternatives providing equivalent service when replacement and discounting assumptions fit. Lowest initial cost is not necessarily lowest annual economic cost.~~The method assumes comparability and often repeatability. Rapid technological change, different capacity or unavailable replacements can make a common-horizon model more appropriate.|Present-value cost: Dated lifecycle outflows valued today.;;Equivalent annual cost: Constant annuity equivalent to that PV.;;Replacement-chain approach: Explicit repeated alternatives over a common horizon.|Assume lifecycle PV cost ₹100 lakh for a five-year asset and a 10% rate.~~Five-year annuity factor = [1 − 1.1^−5]/0.10 ≈ 3.7908.;;EAC = ₹100/3.7908 ≈ ₹26.38 per year.;;Compare with another asset using its own life but equivalent service and defensible replacement assumptions.~~EAC standardises cost per year rather than ignoring lifespan.~~It is not the actual annual cash payment schedule.|Match service outputs.;;Include all lifecycle costs.;;State replacement and horizon assumptions.
T12.10|Capital rationing limits the investments that can be funded, whether through external constraints or internal policy. The objective becomes selecting the highest-value feasible combination, not accepting every positive-NPV proposal. Timing, indivisibility and shared resources complicate simple rankings.~~A profitability-index ranking can help with divisible investments under restrictive assumptions. For indivisible projects, explicit combinations or optimisation may give a better result.|Hard rationing: External funding or other unavoidable limit.;;Soft rationing: Management-imposed budget.;;Divisible versus indivisible: Investment can or cannot be scaled smoothly.|Budget is ₹100 lakh. A costs ₹60 with NPV ₹30; B costs ₹50 with NPV ₹28; C costs ₹40 with NPV ₹25. Assume independent indivisible projects.~~A + C costs ₹100 and adds ₹55.;;B + C costs ₹90 and adds ₹53.;;A + B exceeds the budget; choose A + C among these combinations.~~Feasible total value matters more than a single project's ranking.~~Interdependencies or multiple-period budgets require a richer model.|Identify binding constraints.;;Evaluate combinations and scale.;;Include project interactions and timing.
T12.11|Project risk concerns uncertainty in demand, price, cost, implementation and funding. Scenario analysis combines coherent changes; sensitivity isolates one assumption; probability-weighted evaluation requires defensible likelihoods. Risk should not be hidden in an unexplained discount-rate increase alone.~~Different projects can fail through different mechanisms. A delay may increase construction cost, postpone revenue and create financing stress simultaneously.|Commercial risk: Demand, price and competition.;;Execution risk: Delay, cost and technical performance.;;Financial risk: Liquidity, funding and contractual constraints.|A project's base NPV is ₹20 lakh. Assume a downside with delayed opening and higher cost has NPV −₹30; probabilities are 70% and 30%.~~Expected NPV = 0.7 × ₹20 + 0.3 × (−₹30) = ₹5.;;Inspect whether the ₹30 loss can be financed and survived.;;Test probability estimates and possible mitigation.~~Positive expected value does not eliminate downside funding risk.~~These probabilities are illustrative, not measured evidence.|Identify failure mechanisms.;;Build internally consistent scenarios.;;Assess loss capacity and mitigation alongside expected value.
T12.12|Real options are opportunities to change a project as information arrives, such as delaying, expanding, abandoning or switching inputs. Flexibility can create value when uncertainty is material and decisions are partly irreversible.~~An option is valuable only if the right is practical and protected enough to exercise. Competition, contractual deadlines or large switching costs can erode the apparent flexibility. A strategic label alone is not an option valuation.|Delay: Wait before committing.;;Expand: Increase scale after favourable evidence.;;Abandon: Limit losses by exiting.;;Switch: Change inputs, outputs or operating mode.|A ₹5 lakh pilot reveals demand before a ₹100 expansion. Assume the pilot enables avoiding a weak-market loss of ₹40 in 30% of outcomes, with no other effects.~~Simplified expected avoided loss = 30% × ₹40 = ₹12.;;Subtract pilot cost ₹5 for an illustrative net flexibility benefit ₹7.;;Check whether learning is reliable and expansion can truly be deferred.~~Information plus a usable decision right can improve the investment choice.~~A full option model needs dates, contingent payoffs and costs.|Identify the actual right.;;Model decisions after new information.;;Include competition, exercise and implementation constraints.
T12.13|Dividends distribute cash pro rata under declared terms; repurchases buy shares from participating sellers. Both reduce company cash, while their effects on share count, taxes and ownership differ. A buyback can create or destroy remaining-holder value depending on price and funding.~~Increasing EPS through fewer shares does not automatically increase economic value. Evaluate what cash could earn elsewhere and whether repurchase price differs from a defensible value estimate.|Dividend: Cash distribution without that action reducing share count.;;Repurchase: Cash used to acquire shares.;;Retention: Cash kept for investment or resilience.|A firm has equity value ₹1,000 lakh and 100 shares under a simplified fair-value model. It repurchases 10 shares at ₹10 each.~~Cash paid is ₹100; remaining equity value is ₹900.;;Shares fall to 90.;;Value per remaining share stays ₹10 before taxes, costs and other effects.~~A fair-price distribution does not mechanically create extra per-share value.~~Mispricing, taxes, leverage and investment consequences can change the result.|Compare payout with reinvestment.;;Assess price and funding.;;Separate EPS optics from value per share.
T12.14|Organic growth develops activities internally; acquisitions buy existing capabilities, customers or assets. Each has different timing, execution and integration risks. Acquisition price can transfer anticipated benefits to the seller, so a strategically attractive target need not be a financially attractive deal.~~Compare total incremental cash flows, not just headline speed. Organic projects can fail through ramp-up; acquisitions can fail through overpayment, integration and hidden obligations.|Organic build: Develop resources internally.;;Acquisition: Purchase an existing business or asset base.;;Partnership: Share capabilities and economics through an agreement.|Internal expansion has benefit PV ₹100 lakh and cost ₹70. A target offers the same benefit PV but costs ₹95 plus ₹10 integration.~~Organic NPV = ₹30.;;Acquisition NPV = ₹100 − ₹95 − ₹10 = −₹5.;;Assess speed, uncertainty and other incremental benefits before choosing.~~Similar strategic outputs can have very different value economics.~~The comparison requires realistic timing and comparable capabilities.|Define equivalent strategic outcomes.;;Include full costs and timing.;;Assess execution risks and alternatives.
T12.15|Capital-allocation performance compares investment promises with realised returns, cash and opportunity costs. Incentives based only on size or short-term EPS can encourage overinvestment, underinvestment or acquisitions that improve accounting metrics without adding value.~~Use consistent post-audits and distinguish bad luck from flawed assumptions or incentives. A disciplined process records the original case before outcomes become known, limiting retrospective rewriting.|Project post-audit: Compare forecasts with outcomes.;;Return discipline: Assess incremental capital and value.;;Incentive design: Reward relevant long-term outcomes with controls.|A project was approved for ₹50 lakh investment and ₹10 annual NOPAT, but actual investment reaches ₹80 and NOPAT ₹8.~~Forecast return was 20%.;;Actual simplified return is 8/80 = 10%.;;At a 12% hurdle, investigate overruns, demand and decision governance.~~Both the earnings shortfall and larger capital base weaken results.~~Timing, ramp-up and external shocks should be analysed before assigning responsibility.|Preserve approval assumptions.;;Evaluate cash, capital and returns.;;Design incentives for durable value and honest learning.
